### Round 1: Supply Chain & Registry Lifecycle

* **Generative Dreamer (DMN, T=0.85):**
  > *"The switch to `gcr.io` should be celebrated as a dynamic decoupling vector. By falling back to universal multi-region storage endpoints, we liberate the build pipeline from rigid regional topologies. We can treat `gcr.io` as a generic, protocol-agnostic OCI registry layer. If org constraints lock Artifact Registry in `europe-west3`, our build daemon effortlessly transcodes image references across any accessible GCS-backed endpoint, spawning self-assembling MCP instances anywhere on Earth."*

* **Evaluative Arbiter (Prefrontal Zeno Critic, T=0.20):**
  > *"A severe failure mode is being masked as flexibility. Google Container Registry is officially deprecated and frozen. Storing production MCP images in `gcr.io` introduces critical technical debt:
  > 1. GCR relies on legacy GCS bucket ACLs (`roles/storage.admin` / `roles/storage.objectViewer`), violating the principle of least privilege.
  > 2. Enterprise org policies that ban AR will soon enforce automated GCR shutdown or redirection policies (`pkg.dev` redirection). When the redirect kicks in without the necessary Artifact Registry API permissions, Cloud Build will instantly fail with `403 Forbidden` (`artifactregistry.googleapis.com` disabled).
  > 3. Artifact Registry provides package-level IAM and granular write/read isolation (`roles/artifactregistry.writer`), whereas GCR allows anyone with storage bucket access to tamper with image tags."*

### Round 2: Data Residency & Latency Bounds

* **Generative Dreamer (DMN, T=0.85):**
  > *"Storage latency is negligible in modern fiber meshes. Cloud Run scales horizontally, and once cached on the Borg host node, the image layers are hot. We can construct an adaptive, speculative pre-warming orchestrator over WebSockets where client intent triggers micro-pulls across edge compute nodes before the user even issues an SSH or SQL tool invocation."*

* **Evaluative Arbiter (Prefrontal Zeno Critic, T=0.20):**
  > *"False premise. Consider the physical constraints:
  > 1. `gcr.io` maps to the US multi-region bucket (`us.artifacts.<project-id>.appspot.com`). If the target Cloud Run service is in `europe-west1` (or if fallback fails back to US while databases reside in EU), every cold start incurs cross-Atlantic layer pulls, adding 800ms–2400ms to container startup.
  > 2. `constraints/gcp.resourceLocations` is an enterprise hard stop. An organization blocking Artifact Registry in EU due to location policies will flag US multi-region GCS bucket generation by GCR as an audit violation under GDPR Article 44.
  > 3. Cloud Run's concurrency model for this MCP server (`src/server.ts`) handles WebSocket connections via stateful node instances. Slow cold starts directly translate into MCP client protocol timeouts on Claude Desktop or IDE clients."*

### Round 3: Cloud Run Ephemerality & State Synchronization

* **Generative Dreamer (DMN, T=0.85):**
  > *"Let us evolve the MCP server into a fully fluid, distributed SSH/DB multiplexer. Instead of static container bounds, ephemeral Cloud Run workers can delegate SSH sockets over WebRTC or distributed pub/sub memory buses, allowing any transient instance to pick up an in-flight database transaction or terminal session."*

* **Evaluative Arbiter (Prefrontal Zeno Critic, T=0.20):**
  > *"Inspect the actual codebase in `src/ssh-tools.ts` and `src/server.ts`:
  > - Line 9 of `src/ssh-tools.ts` creates a singleton `NodeSSH` client held directly in local memory.
  > - Line 84 of `src/server.ts` binds WebSocket connections to in-memory state.
  > - Cloud Run scales down to 0 instances when idle (`--max-instances 10`, `--min-instances 0` by default).
  > If a Cloud Run instance terminates during an active SSH session or MySQL tunnel, state is lost instantly with zero checkpointing. Layering a registry regression on top of an unbuffered stateful protocol amplifies transport failure rates. We must establish rigorous session affinity, state externalization, and pre-flight capability negotiation."*

---

