## 5. Offline Sync, Caching & Incremental Perturbation

```
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│               User Interaction Event                   │
│         (Call, Meeting, WhatsApp, Deal Update)         │
└───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│            Room / SwiftData Transaction                │
│    • Set contact_graph_edges.is_dirty = TRUE           │
│    • Invalidate Local Delta Cache                      │
└───────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┘
                            │
              Is Full Recalculation Scheduled?
             ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
            YES                            NO
             │                             │
             ▼                             ▼
┌───────────────────────────┐ ┌───────────────────────────┐
│ WorkManager / BGTask      │ │ Real-Time UI Query:       │
│ • Periodic (Charging+Idle)│ │ • First-Order Perturb.    │
│ • Full Spectral Solver    │ │ • Use Cached Eigenvectors │
│ • Clean is_dirty flags    │ │ • Zero UI Jitter / Frame  │
└───────────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────────┘
```

### 5.1 Real-Time First-Order Perturbation
When an edge weight shifts by $\Delta W_{uv}$, calculating new eigenvectors from scratch on the main thread is forbidden. We calculate the first-order eigenvalue shift:

$$\Delta \lambda_k \approx \mathbf{v}_k^T (\Delta L_{\text{sym}}) \mathbf{v}_k$$

This reduces runtime complexity from $\mathcal{O}(N^3)$ to $\mathcal{O}(K \cdot \text{deg}(u))$, executing in $<0.5\,\text{ms}$ on mobile CPU threads.

### 5.2 Android WorkManager Periodic Background Worker

```kotlin
package com.netplanner.worker

import android.content.Context
import androidx.work.Constraints
import androidx.work.CoroutineWorker
import androidx.work.PeriodicWorkRequestBuilder
import androidx.work.WorkerParameters
import com.netplanner.data.local.dao.ContactGraphDao
import kotlinx.coroutines.Dispatchers
import kotlinx.coroutines.withContext
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit

class NetworkGraphSpectralWorker(
    appContext: Context,
    workerParams: WorkerParameters,
    private val graphDao: ContactGraphDao
) : CoroutineWorker(appContext, workerParams) {

    override suspend fun doWork(): Result = withContext(Dispatchers.Default) {
        try {
            // Check dirty status before running expensive decomposition
            val dirtyEdgeCount = graphDao.getDirtyEdgeCount()
            if (dirtyEdgeCount == 0) return@withContext Result.success()

            val contacts = graphDao.getAllContactsForGraph()
            if (contacts.size < 3) return@withContext Result.success()

            val edges = graphDao.getAllActiveEdges()

            // Build Sparse Adjacency and Solve
            val solver = SpectralSolverAndroid(maxClusters = 6)
            val clusteringOutput = solver.computeClusters(contacts, edges)

            // Persist clusters and VIP isolation metrics atomically
            graphDao.updateSpectralClustersTransaction(
                assignments = clusteringOutput.assignments,
                vipIsolations = clusteringOutput.vipIsolations
            )

            graphDao.clearDirtyFlags()
            Result.success()
        } catch (e: Exception) {
            Result.retry()
        }
    }

    companion object {
        fun buildPeriodicRequest() = PeriodicWorkRequestBuilder<NetworkGraphSpectralWorker>(
            repeatInterval = 24,
            repeatIntervalTimeUnit = TimeUnit.HOURS
        ).setConstraints(
            Constraints.Builder()
                .setRequiresCharging(true)
                .setRequiresDeviceIdle(true)
                .build()
        ).build()
    }
}
```

---

## 6. Edge Cases, Failure Modes & Safety Bounds

| Edge Case | Failure Mode | Mitigation Strategy |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Fully Disconnected Multi-Graph** | Multiplicity of $\lambda_0 = 0$ is equal to number of components $C > 1$, breaking standard single-component assumption. | Extract Connected Components via Disjoint-Set (Union-Find) in $\mathcal{O}(V + E \cdot \alpha(V))$. Run spectral decomposition only on components with $|V| \ge 4$; mark isolated nodes directly with $C(u) = 0$. |
| **Star Topology / Super-Hub Node** | The user or an executive assistant connects to every single contact, skewing $D^{-1/2}$ and collapsing all embeddings onto a single axis. | Remove the self-root (User ID) from the adjacency graph prior to Laplacian synthesis. Normalize degree vectors using soft-thresholding: $\tilde{D}_{uu} = \min(D_{uu}, 3\sigma_D)$. |
| **Graph Explosion ($N > 5{,}000$)** | Dense matrix allocation causes Out-Of-Memory (OOM) crashes in mobile virtual memory. | Filter graph to active/dormant contacts having $\text{strategicScore} > 0.15$ or interaction within past 365 days; bound active on-device solving graph to top $N=2{,}000$ nodes. |
| **Metric Exploitation (Goodhart's Law)** | An artificial contact added with maximum manual rating without interactions falsely triggers constant high-priority alerts. | Require two independent physical interaction channels (e.g., recorded CallLog + Calendar event or WhatsApp thread) before a contact can exceed $S_u > 0.60$. |

---

## 7. Performance & Battery Safety Verification

1. **Memory Ceiling**: Working memory must not exceed $12\,\text{MB}$ heap allocation during decomposition at $N = 2{,}000$.
2. **CPU Execution Budget**:
   * iOS: `ssyev_` completes in $\le 42\,\text{ms}$ on Apple Silicon A16/A17/M-series for $N=1{,}000$.
   * Android: Native SIMD solver completes in $\le 110\,\text{ms}$ on Snapdragon 8 Gen 2/3 and Tensor G3 for $N=1{,}000$.
3. **Database Concurrency**: Graph edge writes must use batch transactions (`@Transaction` in Room / `ModelContext.transaction` in SwiftData) with `SQLCipher` page cache size pinned to $2\,\text{MB}$ to eliminate disk I/O bottlenecks.

